WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.410
My pupils sent a text message in

2
00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:07.430
Arabic, and it went as three messages.

3
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.540
The same text in English went as one.

4
00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.450
Same phone, same length,

5
00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.890
three times the price.

6
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.160
The reason is bits.

7
00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.350
Today: bits and bytes, how every device

8
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.390
stores numbers, words and sensor readings.

9
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.600
Inside a device, everything is stored as

10
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.580
tiny switches that are on or off.

11
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:40.350
Each one is a bit: a one or a zero.

12
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.960
One bit can only say yes or no.

13
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.950
But every extra bit doubles how

14
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.110
many values you can make.

15
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.940
In binary, each place is worth double

16
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.880
the one to its right: one, two, four,

17
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.920
eight, sixteen, and so on.

18
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.470
To read a binary number, add up

19
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.610
the places that are on.

20
00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.530
One, zero, one means four,

21
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.370
no two, and one: five.

22
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.550
Eight bits make a byte, which

23
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.110
holds any number from zero to two

24
00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:16.090
hundred and fifty-five.

25
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.420
A Latin letter, like A, is

26
00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:20.600
stored in one byte.

27
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.440
An Arabic letter needs two bytes.

28
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.580
A text message has room for a hundred

29
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.520
and forty bytes, so an Arabic one

30
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.200
holds only seventy letters.

31
00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:32.990
Quick check!

32
00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.650
In binary, what number is one, one, zero?

33
00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:39.230
Six.

34
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.070
The places are four, two and one.

35
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.640
Four plus two is six, and

36
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.000
the ones place is zero.

37
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.120
Bits matter for sensors too.

38
00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.610
A microcontroller turns a

39
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.710
sensor's voltage into a number.

40
00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.940
An Arduino Uno uses ten bits, so

41
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.200
its readings go from zero to one

42
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.480
thousand and twenty-three.

43
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:07.790
An ESP32 uses twelve bits: from

44
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.430
zero to four thousand and

45
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.350
ninety-five, so it sees finer steps.

46
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.010
What does a step mean?

47
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.340
On a five-volt Arduino, each step

48
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.220
is about five millivolts.

49
00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.520
With a common temperature sensor,

50
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.960
that's about half a degree per step.

51
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.300
More bits give finer steps, but

52
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.000
more data to store and send.

53
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.360
So how big is data?

54
00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.330
A temperature reading fits in two bytes.

55
00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.950
A text message is about a

56
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:39.590
hundred and forty.

57
00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.770
A phone photo is about three million.

58
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.320
That's why a sensor can send readings all

59
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.900
year over a slow, cheap network.

60
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:50.750
Your turn.

61
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.240
Our weather station sends one

62
00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.140
temperature reading every ten

63
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.640
minutes, over mobile data.

64
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.020
Will that use a lot of data?

65
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.670
No: about a hundred and fifty readings a

66
00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:06.290
day, a few bytes each, is tiny, even with

67
00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.130
the extra bytes every message carries.

68
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:13.350
Remember: a bit is one or zero, each

69
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.670
place doubles, eight bits make a byte,

70
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.350
and more bits give finer readings.

71
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.090
Try it in class tomorrow:

72
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:23.910
binary dot cards.

73
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.660
Give each group five cards, and race to

74
00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:28.940
make the numbers you call out.

75
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.530
Finish with every pupil

76
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.570
writing their age in binary.

77
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.560
Next time: the microcontroller

78
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.320
that puts all of this to work.
